# Findings the full-history secret scan raises that are not secrets.
#
# Each line is a gitleaks FINGERPRINT - commit:file:rule:line - so it suppresses
# exactly one finding in exactly one historical commit. No rule is disabled and
# no forward blind spot is created: the same rule firing on a new commit, or on
# a different line of the same file, is a new fingerprint and still fails.
#
# WHY THERE ARE ENTRIES HERE AT ALL. The CI secret scan was decorative until
# 2026-09-14 - it ran gitleaks with --staged on a checkout that stages nothing,
# so it reported "0 commits scanned ... no leaks found" on every run. Its first
# real pass over 221 commits raised these two. Both are documentation, not
# credentials.
#
# 816d664b (2025-10-18) SECURITY.md:197 and 86f97a0c (2025-10-12)
# docs/SECURITY.md:157 are the SAME example, before and after the docs/ move: a
# PowerShell snippet inside a code block headed "[-] BAD: Plain text in
# variables", showing an api-key assignment as the thing NOT to do. The string
# is an illustration written for the page; it never authenticated to anything.
# Neither file carries it today - the example was dropped from the current tree
# - so these are purely historical and cannot recur.
#
# [!] Before adding a line here, check which it is. A fingerprint for a REAL
# credential is the wrong fix: revoke or rotate it first, per the code-two
# policy on committed secrets. An entry in this file says "this was never a
# secret", not "this secret is handled".

